---
components: ["Histogram"]
description: Histogram counts values into bins over a numeric axis, to show the shape of a distribution.
---

<script>
  import { ChartThreshold, Histogram } from "carbon-components-svelte/viz";

  // A right-skewed sample, like request latencies.
  const requests = Array.from({ length: 1200 }, (_, i) => {
    const u = ((i * 7919) % 1200) / 1200 + 0.0004;
    return { latencyMs: Math.round(20 + -70 * Math.log(1 - u * 0.985)), route: i % 3 ? "/api" : "/search" };
  });
  const sorted = requests.map((row) => row.latencyMs).sort((a, b) => a - b);
  const p50 = sorted[Math.floor(sorted.length * 0.5)];
  const p95 = sorted[Math.floor(sorted.length * 0.95)];
</script>

`Histogram` is part of the data visualization subpackage. Import it from `carbon-components-svelte/viz` and load `carbon-components-svelte/css/viz.css` alongside `all.css`. With a single-theme stylesheet such as `g100.css`, load the matching `viz-g100.css` instead. The subpackage is experimental: its API may change in a minor release.

It is a [Chart](/components/Chart) preset. For a table cell, use [MicroHistogram](/components/MicroHistogram).

## Basic

Pass `data` and an `x` accessor. Values are counted into bins whose bars touch, because the axis is continuous. Hover or focus a bin to read its range and count.

<Histogram data={requests} x="latencyMs" title="Request latency" xTitle="Milliseconds" yTitle="Requests" />

## Bins

Set `bins` to a target count or to a rule: `"sturges"` (the default) or `"freedman-diaconis"`, which suits skewed data. Bin edges snap to round numbers, so the count may shift. Set `domain` to bin over a fixed range.

<Histogram data={requests} x="latencyMs" bins={40} title="40 bins" height={220} />

<Histogram data={requests} x="latencyMs" bins={12} domain={[0, 600]} title="Fixed range" height={220} />

### Counted elsewhere

Pass a list of `{ x0, x1, count }` to `bins` when the counting happens on the server. `data` and `x` are then ignored.

<Histogram
  bins={[
    { x0: 0, x1: 20, count: 14 },
    { x0: 20, x1: 40, count: 62 },
    { x0: 40, x1: 60, count: 118 },
    { x0: 60, x1: 80, count: 74 },
    { x0: 80, x1: 100, count: 21 },
  ]}
  title="Order value"
  xTitle="USD"
  height={220}
/>

## Markers

Set `markers` to values to call out, such as percentiles. A marker is a labelled rule that never grows the axis: outside the range it is not drawn. Other marks, such as a `ChartThreshold`, go in the default slot.

<Histogram
  data={requests}
  x="latencyMs"
  bins={30}
  markers={[{ x: p50, label: "p50" }, { x: p95, label: "p95" }]}
  title="Request latency with percentiles"
  height={240}
>
  <ChartThreshold y={150} label="Alert volume" kind="warning" />
</Histogram>

## Labels and formats

`seriesLabel` names the count in the tooltip and the table. `rangeFormat` writes a bin's range, and `yFormat` the count.

<Histogram
  data={requests}
  x="latencyMs"
  seriesLabel="Requests"
  rangeFormat={(from, to) => `${from} to ${to} ms`}
  title="Request latency"
  height={220}
/>

## Color

Set `color` to a semantic name, a categorical index from 1 to 14, a viz token name, or any CSS color.

<Histogram data={requests} x="latencyMs" color="neutral" title="Request latency" height={220} />

## Data table

Set `toolbar` to let people swap the chart for a table of the bins, and export it.

<Histogram data={requests} x="latencyMs" seriesLabel="Requests" title="Request latency" toolbar height={220} />

## Composition

`Histogram` is `ChartBins` inside a `Chart` whose rows are bins. Compose them yourself for anything the preset does not cover: `ChartBins` draws any rows that carry numeric `x0` and `x1`, and `ChartMarker` works in any chart. Both are listed with the other primitives on the [Chart](/components/Chart) page.

## Accessibility

The chart is a single tab stop. Left and Right move between bins, Enter or Space selects, and Escape clears. The focused bin is announced with its range and count, such as "40–60: Count 118", and the other bins are dimmed rather than recoloring the focused one. The data table view lists every bin.
